<resources>
    <color name="colorBorder">#E8E8E8</color>
    <color name="colorUrlBarBackground">#E8E8E8</color>

    <!--
      Accent colors for the "Interface color" picker live in gen_accent.xml (generated
      theme variants, see AccentTheme). The default is spelled out here instead, because the
      framework's own accent (purple) would otherwise show wherever the theme is not
      overridden, and it is not what the picker offers as its default.

      It sits off the generated grid: a soft champagne gold rather than one of the 1152
      representable accents. That is affordable because App.Theme is the one theme whose
      colorPrimary is written literally, and AccentTheme.variantFor() answers with it for
      exactly this ARGB - see the note there about the picker only reaching this color by
      code, never by dragging.

      The variant darkens the accent the way gen_accent.xml does it: the HSV value is scaled
      by 0.78125 before the channels are rounded down, which on an already-quantized triple
      costs a unit here and there (#FFFFD400 goes to #FFC7A600, not C7A5). Applying that
      factor straight to this color's channels and rounding down is the closest match.
    -->
    <color name="accent_default">#FFEECE6D</color>
    <color name="accent_default_dark">#FFB9A055</color>

    <!-- Fill of the Wake-on-LAN chip on a server card: neutral gray in both themes, so
         the button reads as an action without borrowing any accent colour. -->
    <color name="colorWakeButton">#E8E8E8</color>

    <!-- Floating mini window. These are deliberately theme independent (no values-night
         counterpart): the window floats over somebody else's app, so a panel that flipped
         with our own light/dark setting would either vanish against a white page or glare
         on a black one. The set matches the upstream Easycontrol mini window, which this
         one is modelled on - white card, near-black icons, blue drag handle, red dismiss.
         -->
    <color name="colorMiniCard">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="colorMiniOnCard">#1A1A1A</color>
    <color name="colorMiniBar">#579CF9</color>
    <color name="colorMiniAlert">#CA5357</color>
    <color name="colorMiniHandle">#66808080</color>

    <!-- Floating assist ball. Theme independent for the same reason as the mini window: it
         floats over the remote picture rather than over our own chrome. A plain black disc
         with no glyph - the ball has to read as an object on any stream, not pick up a theme
         colour that could match the desktop behind it. -->
    <color name="colorAssistBall">#000000</color>

    <!--
      Full QWERTY keyboard. Deliberately theme independent (no values-night counterpart):
      the requested look is a fixed black board - black keycaps separated by gray hairline
      borders, white labels, black top/grip bar - so it must not drift with the app's
      light/dark or accent setting the way the surface/onSurface pair used to.

      Pressed and latched (checked) caps lighten to a dark gray so a keypress still reads
      as feedback against the black, while the white label stays legible on top.
    -->
    <color name="colorFullKbBg">#000000</color>
    <color name="colorFullKbKey">#000000</color>
    <color name="colorFullKbKeyPressed">#3C3C3C</color>
    <color name="colorFullKbBorder">#808080</color>
    <color name="colorFullKbText">#FFFFFF</color>
</resources>